# Docs move with the code

## Pyramid Layer

- Layer: L1 core rule.

## Use This When

- Any change that alters what a user or client observes: tool contracts and
  error codes, write/read defaults, CLI commands and flags, env vars, install
  or deploy steps, dashboard screens, health endpoints, version numbers.

## Stop Here If

- The change is purely internal (refactor, test, infra plumbing) and no page
  in `docs/` makes a claim it invalidates.

## The rule

- The documentation site (`docs/*.mdx` + `apps/docs`) states facts about the
  code. A change that invalidates such a fact updates the page **in the same
  epic branch**, not in a later cleanup — the same lockstep already required
  for package READMEs.
- Contract-shape changes and behavioural changes a client can observe also
  land in `docs/DEPRECATIONS.md` (behavioural changes have a dedicated
  section there).
- Periodic full reconciliation is owned by the **docs-auditor agent** (a
  local agent definition; ask for "audit the docs"). Invoke it after an epic
  merges into `dev` and before cutting a release; its commits use the
  `docs(audit):` prefix, which is the anchor the next audit resumes from.
- Docs content is English; MCP tools are never rendered as terminal commands;
  committed pages carry no private planning references.
